There are absolute constants $0<\beta,\eta_0<1$ and $C_0,C_1\ge1$ with the following property. Let $f$ be an order-$k$ feature from <a href="#eq:feature" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:feature">[eq:feature]</a>, and let $\Delta$ arise from a moment functional of order $2D$ with the prefix conditions of Section <a href="#sec:setup" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:setup">2</a>. Suppose $$0<S\le4,\qquad 0<S'\le\min\{1,\eta_0S\},\qquad 4k\le D,$$ and, for some $\beta S\le s\le S$ and $\lvert \omega\rvert\le2.002s$, $$\label{eq:multiscale-cost}
 \lvert \mathcal S_f(s,\omega)\rvert\ge e^{-E},\qquad
 E\ge C_0\left(1+k\log(2k)+\log H_f+\log\frac1{S'}\right).$$ Then an order-$k'$ feature $f'$, with $1\le k'\le4k$, satisfies $$\label{eq:multiscale-output}
 \lvert \mathcal S_{f'}(s',\omega')\rvert\ge e^{-7.98E},\qquad
 \beta S'\le s'\le S',\qquad \lvert \omega'\rvert\le2.002s',$$ and $$\label{eq:multiscale-height}
 H_{f'}\le H_f^4\left(1+\frac1{\beta S'}\right)^3.$$ Moreover, there are $1\le m\le4$ lists, each equal to the old parameter list $(z_1,\ldots,z_{k-1})$ or its complex conjugate, and at most $m-1$ added parameters, with the following property. Each new inner-gap parameter is a nonempty product using at most one entry from each list and at most one added parameter. Every added parameter $e^{-a+\mathrm i\theta}$ obeys $$\label{eq:added-parameters}
 a\ge\beta S',\qquad \lvert \theta\rvert\le C_1S.$$

Let $s_1,E>0$, let $\lvert \omega\rvert\le2.003s_1$, and let $\Lambda:\mathbb R\to[0,\infty]$ be measurable and satisfy <a href="#eq:poisson-log-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:poisson-log-bound">[eq:poisson-log-bound]</a>. There is a real $\lambda$ with $$\label{eq:single-frequency}
 \lvert \lambda\rvert\le2s_1,\qquad \Lambda(\lambda)\le2.40E.$$ There is also an absolute $C_2$ such that, for every $\lvert y\rvert\le2s_1$, some real $t,t'$ satisfy $$\label{eq:paired-frequencies}
 t-t'=y,\qquad \lvert t\rvert,\lvert t'\rvert\le C_2s_1,\qquad
 \Lambda(t)+\Lambda(t')\le2.74E.$$
